Brute Force – Store elements and sort whenever needed
### **Complexity Analysis**

- **addNum():** O(1)
- **findMedian():** O(n log n)
- **Space Complexity:** O(n)

**Note:** This approach is simple but inefficient because it sorts the entire array every time the median is requested.

class MedianFinder {
    vector<int> nums;

public:
    MedianFinder() {
    }

    void addNum(int num) {
        nums.push_back(num);
    }

    double findMedian() {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        int n = nums.size();

        if (n % 2 == 1) {
            return nums[n / 2];
        }

        return ((double)nums[n / 2 - 1] + nums[n / 2]) / 2.0;
    }
};


Better – Maintain a sorted vector
Instead of sorting the entire vector every time, we can keep it sorted while inserting new elements.

Idea:

Use lower_bound() to find the correct insertion position and insert the new number there.
### **Complexity Analysis**

- **addNum():** O(n), because vector elements may need to be shifted when inserting an element.
- **findMedian():** O(1), because the median can be accessed directly.
- **Space Complexity:** O(n).

**Note:** This approach is more efficient than sorting the entire array repeatedly because the median can be retrieved in constant time.
class MedianFinder {
    vector<int> nums;

public:
    MedianFinder() {
    }

    void addNum(int num) {
        auto it = lower_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), num);
        nums.insert(it, num);
    }

    double findMedian() {
        int n = nums.size();

        if (n % 2 == 1) {
            return nums[n / 2];
        }

        return ((double)nums[n / 2 - 1] + nums[n / 2]) / 2.0;
    }
};



Optimal – Two Heaps (Max Heap + Min Heap)
Best approach for interviews
Key intuition
Instead of sorting all elements, divide the incoming numbers into two halves.
- Max Heap (left): Stores the smaller half of the numbers. Its top gives the largest element in the smaller half.
- Min Heap (right): Stores the larger half of the numbers. Its top gives the smallest element in the larger half.

class MedianFinder {
    // Max heap: smaller half
    priority_queue<int> left;

    // Min heap: larger half
    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> right;

public:
    MedianFinder() {
    }

    void addNum(int num) {

        // Insert into the appropriate half
        if (left.empty() || num <= left.top()) {
            left.push(num);
        }
        else {
            right.push(num);
        }

        // Balance the heaps
        if (left.size() > right.size() + 1) {
            right.push(left.top());
            left.pop();
        }
        else if (right.size() > left.size()) {
            left.push(right.top());
            right.pop();
        }
    }

    double findMedian() {

        if (left.size() > right.size()) {
            return left.top();
        }

        return ((double)left.top() + right.top()) / 2.0;
    }
};
### **Why?**

- **Insertion into a heap:** O(log n), because inserting an element may require moving it up the heap to maintain the heap property.
- **Rebalancing:** O(log n) in the worst case, because at most one element is transferred between the two heaps. The transfer involves a pop and a push.
- **Accessing the top element:** O(1), because the root element is directly accessible.
- **Space Complexity:** O(n), because both heaps together store all n elements.
